// The search engines offered in settings.
//
// id       -- what settings.json stores and what the page sends back.
//             Never change one: an id that disappears turns a saved
//             preference into "unknown engine" and silently resets it.
// label    -- what the settings page shows.
// template -- the query URL. Exactly one %s, replaced with the
//             percent-encoded query.
//
// The first entry is the default a fresh profile is created with.
// DuckDuckGo, and not because anybody paid for it: a browser that says it
// makes no request the user did not ask for cannot ship with a default
// that reports every search to an advertising company.
//
// Data, not code. These were three arms of an enum in settings.rs with
// their URLs written into a match, so adding an engine meant editing Rust,
// adding an enum variant, and rebuilding. A profile can also override this
// file: put your own engines.json in the profile directory.
//
// "custom" is not here and cannot be: it is the user's own template, kept
// in settings.json as custom-search-url, and an id by that name is
// rejected on load.
{
  "engine": [
    {
      "id": "duckduckgo",
      "label": "DuckDuckGo",
      "template": "https://duckduckgo.com/?q=%s"
    },
    {
      "id": "google",
      "label": "Google",
      "template": "https://www.google.com/search?q=%s"
    },
    {
      "id": "bing",
      "label": "Bing",
      "template": "https://www.bing.com/search?q=%s"
    }
  ]
}
